6.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The exemplars dad, dab, gut, low, cat are all C V C monosyllables.

* 2nd position is a vowel and in the set {a,o,u}
  dad a, dab a, gut u, low o, cat a
* 3rd position is a consonant and in the set {b,d,t,w}
  dad d, dab b, gut t, low w, cat t

The five negatives ear, cop, ego, mom, ate all break that C V C + 2nd∈{a,o,u} + 3rd∈{b,d,t,w} description:
ear 2nd a ok, 3rd r∉{b,d,t,w}
cop 2nd o ok, 3rd p∉{b,d,t,w}
ego 2nd g∉{a,o,u}
mom 2nd o ok, 3rd m∉{b,d,t,w}
ate 2nd t∉{a,o,u}

On that reading the choice set is:
leg l e g – 2nd e∉{a,o,u}, 3rd g∉{b,d,t,w}
dam d a m – 2nd a ok, 3rd m∉{b,d,t,w}
rat r a t – 2nd a∈{a,o,u}, 3rd t∈{b,d,t,w} → the only option that satisfies the exemplar description
car c a r – 2nd a ok, 3rd r∉{b,d,t,w}
bin b i n – 2nd i∉{a,o,u}, 3rd n∉{b,d,t,w}

So rat is the unique member of the dad/dab/gut/low/cat family in the list, and leg, dam, car, bin are non-members. Leg is the canonical non-member quoted by several colleagues because it introduces the forbidden vowel e and ends in g∉{b,d,t,w}.

A competing letter-avoidance reading in the round is “no e,p,r,m” and the good alphabet {a,b,c,d,g,l,o,t,u,w}. That also separates the exemplars from the negatives, but it would admit i/n and it makes bin look like the only e,r,p,m-free option. That reading is inconsistent with the actual letter inventory of the exemplars – i and n never occur in dad,dab,gut,low,cat – and it is the source of the rat vs bin disagreement in the room. The vowel+final-coda description is the tighter one and makes rat the clear inlier, with leg/dam/car/bin all being non-followers, leg being the textbook e-containing example.

2.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The exemplars are all monosyllabic C V C.

* dad = d a d
* dab = d a b
* gut = g u t
* low = l o w
* cat = c a t

Hence 2nd position is a vowel and 3rd position is a consonant. The vowel set that is actually realised is

2nd ∈ {a,o,u}

and the coda set that is actually realised is

3rd ∈ {b,d,t,w}

The negative exemplars are all outside that C V C a/o/u b/d/t/w family:

ear  e a r  – 3rd r ∉ {b,d,t,w}
cop  c o p  – 3rd p ∉ {b,d/t/w}
ego  e g o  – not C V C, 2nd g ∉ {a,o,u}
mom  m o m  – 3rd m ∉ {b,d,t,w}
ate  a t e  – not C V C, 2nd t ∉ {a,o,u}

That is the positional description that is consistently recovered in the round. It is equivalent to the e-p-r-m-free description that is also quoted, with the extra C V C requirement making ego/ate non-members for structural reasons as well.

On that positional description

* leg = l e g : 2nd e ∉ {a,o,u}, 3rd g ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* dam = d a m : 2nd a ok, 3rd m ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* car = c a r : 2nd a ok, 3rd r ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* bin = b i n : 2nd i ∉ {a,o,u}, 3rd n ∉ {b,d,t,w}

are all non-members of the dad/dab/gut/low/cat family. The only choice that satisfies 2nd ∈ {a,o,u} ∧ 3rd ∈ {b,d,t,w} is

* rat = r a t

so rat is the unique option that would belong with the exemplars, the other four being non-members. In the usual textbook illustration of a non-member leg is the canonical example – it introduces the forbidden vowel e and a coda g ∉ {b,d,t,w}, exactly the ear/cop/ego/mom/ate type of break.

Note the divergence in the round: a looser letter-avoidance reading {no e,p,r,m} makes bin the only e-p-r-m-free option and makes rat, leg, dam, car all non-members. Under that looser reading leg remains the canonical e-containing non-member, and bin is the only choice that can be built from the e-p-r-m-free alphabet. The positional a/o/u + b/d/t/w reading is the more specific and structurally consistent one, and it is the one that makes rat the sole matching option.

12.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The five positives are all monosyllabic C V C.

* dad = d a d
* dab = d a b
* gut = g u t
* low = l o w
* cat = c a t

Hence 2nd letter ∈ {a,o,u} and 3rd letter ∈ {b,d,t,w}. That positional description is sufficient for the five negatives:

ear  e a r → 3rd r ∉ {b,d,t,w}
cop  c o p → 3rd p ∉ {b,d,t,w}
ego  e g o → 2nd g ∉ {a,o,u}
mom  m o m → 3rd m ∉ {b,d,t,w}
ate  a t e → 2nd t ∉ {a,o,u}

The five negatives are also exactly the e/r/p/m-contaminated items. The exemplars can be described as the e,r,p,m-free alphabet {a,b,c,d,g,l,o,t,u,w} C V C.

On the positional reading the options break down as

leg = l e g : 2nd e ∉ {a,o,u}, 3rd g ∉ {b,d,t,w}
dam = d a m : 2nd a ok, 3rd m ∉ {b,d,t,w}
rat = r a t : 2nd a ∈ {a,o,u}, 3rd t ∈ {b,d,t,w} → the only option that satisfies the 2nd/3rd condition
car = c a r : 2nd a ok, 3rd r ∉ {b,d,t,w}
bin = b i n : 2nd i ∉ {a,o,u}, 3rd n ∉ {b,d,t,w}

So under the 2nd∈{a,o,u} ∧ 3rd∈{b,d,t,w} reading rat is the unique member of the set A–E and leg/dam/car/bin are non-members. Leg is the canonical textbook non-member because it introduces the forbidden vowel e and a forbidden coda g.

On the looser e,r,p,m-avoidance reading the exemplars are the e,r,p,m-free C V C words. Then

leg contains e, dam contains m, rat contains r, car contains r

are all e/r/p/m contaminated like the five negatives, whereas

bin = b i n

contains no e,r,p,m at all and is the only option that can be built from the good alphabet. That is the source of the bin-as-member vs rat-as-member disagreement in the room. The positional condition is more specific than pure letter avoidance because it rules out i and n, e.g. bin, which the avoidance reading would admit.

Current belief: the intended structural pattern is C V C with 2nd∈{a,o,u} and 3rd∈{b,d,t,w}. On that reading rat is the only option that follows the dad/dab/gut/low/cat pattern, and leg, dam, car, bin are non-followers, with leg being the most clearly illustrative non-member. If a single non-member must be named, leg is the conventionally quoted example.

