On that description the choice set evaluates as:
* leg = l e g : 2nd e ∉ {a,o,u}, 3rd g ∉ {b,d,t,w}, sole vowel is E
* dam = d a m : 2nd a ok, 3rd m ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* rat = r a t : 2nd a ∈ {a,o,u}, 3rd t ∈ {b,d,t,w}
* car = c a r : 2nd a ok, 3rd r ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* bin = b i n : 2nd i ∉ {a,o,u}, 3rd n ∉ {b,d,t,w}

So rat is the unique inlier under the strict 2nd/3rd positional rule, and leg/dam/car/bin are all non-members. Leg is the canonical non-member: C V C like the positives but with forbidden vowel E in 2nd place and coda g ∉ {b,d,t,w}, mirroring ear/ego/ate.

The looser e/p/r/m-free reading – positives built from {a,b,c,d,g,l,o,t,u,w} with no e,p,r,m – makes bin the only e/p/r/m-free option in A–E, with leg, dam, rat, car all contaminated. That reading is still present in the discussion but is now seen as overfitting.

Round 2 public responses are unanimous for A. leg as the answer to “which does not follow”. The argument is that rat is the best follower under the coda-set reading, so it cannot be the non-follower; the unique clean break among A–E is leg’s E vowel / forbidden coda. Dam, car, bin also fail the strict positional rule, but leg is the textbook illustration of the e / coda violation shown by the negatives. Consensus now centers on A. leg as the intended canonical non-follower, with rat noted as the inlier under the most structurally specific formulation.

reflection_text.3:
