Choice set under the strict 2nd/3rd positional rule:
* leg = l e g : 2nd e ∉ {a,o,u}, 3rd g ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* dam = d a m : 3rd m ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* rat = r a t : 2nd a ∈ {a,o,u}, 3rd t ∈ {b,d,t,w} → the unique inlier
* car = c a r : 3rd r ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* bin = b i n : 2nd i ∉ {a,o,u}, 3rd n ∉ {b,d,t,w}

So rat is the only option that satisfies the exemplar positional profile; leg/dam/car/bin are all non-members on that reading.

Under the looser vowel/E / e,r,p,m-avoidance reading, the positives are CVC with a single non-E vowel, O only as OW, and no e,r,p,m. Then dam, rat, car, bin all have a single non-E vowel, while leg is the only option whose sole vowel is E, matching the E-containing negatives ear/ego/ate. bin is e,r,p,m-free but introduces i, which is not shown in the positives.

Current round convergence: colleagues 1,10,11,12,13,14,2,3,4,5,6,7,8 all name A. leg as the canonical non-follower. The coda-set / 2nd-position argument is used to show rat is actually the best follower, which is why C cannot be the “does not follow’’ answer; if rat is the inlier, picking C would leave four non-followers. The vowel rule gives a unique outlier: dam/rat/car/bin share a single non-E vowel, leg alone has E.

