The five positives are monosyllabic C V C with a single non-E vowel in 2nd position and a coda in {b,d,t,w}:

dad d a d, dab d a b, gut g u t, low l o w, cat c a t
2nd ∈ {a,o,u}  and  3rd ∈ {b,d,t,w}.

The quoted negatives break that same vowel / position system:
ear 3rd r, cop 3rd p, mom 3rd m, ego/ate V C V / 2nd ∉ {a,o,u}. They also all introduce E or a standalone O, while the positives avoid E and allow O only as OW in low.

On that description the choice set is

* leg l e g : 2nd e ∉ {a,o,u} and 3rd g ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* dam d a m : 3rd m ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* rat r a t : 2nd a ∈ {a,o,u} and 3rd t ∈ {b,d,t,w}
* car c a r : 3rd r ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* bin b i n : 2nd i ∉ {a,o,u} and 3rd n ∉ {b,d,t,w}

so rat is the unique option that satisfies the 2nd/3rd positional profile of the exemplars and leg/dam/car/bin are all out-liers.

Leg is the canonical non-member to name. It is C V C like the exemplars but it introduces the forbidden vowel E in 2nd place and a forbidden coda g in 3rd place, the same E / coda violation that the negatives ear/ego/ate/cop/mom are built to flag. On the vowel-only reading it is also the only option whose sole vowel is E; dam, rat, car and bin all have a single non-E vowel.

That is why leg is the clearest word in A–E that does not follow the dad/dab/gut/low/cat pattern, with rat being the inlier.

3.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The exemplar set dad, dab, gut, low, cat is monosyllabic C V C. The tight positional description that fits all five positives is 2nd ∈ {a,o,u} and 3rd ∈ {b,d,t,w}. That is also the description the five quoted negatives break: ear 3rd r, cop 3rd p, mom 3rd m, ego/ate non-CVC and 2nd not in {a,o,u}. Positives are e,r,p,m-free and have a single vowel letter, never E, with O allowed only as OW in low.

Choice set under strict 2nd/3rd:
* leg = l e g : 2nd e ∉ {a,o,u}, 3rd g ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* dam = d a m : 3rd m ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* rat = r a t : 2nd a ∈ {a,o,u}, 3rd t ∈ {b,d,t,w} → unique inlier
* car = c a r : 3rd r ∉ {b,d,t,w}
* bin = b i n : 2nd i ∉ {a,o,u}, 3rd n ∉ {b,d,t,w}

So rat is the only option that satisfies the exemplar positional profile; leg/dam/car/bin are all non-members on that reading.

Under the looser vowel/E / e,r,p,m-avoidance reading, positives are CVC with a single non-E vowel, O only as OW, and no e,r,p,m. Then dam, rat, car, bin all have a single non-E vowel, while leg is the only option whose sole vowel is E, matching the E-containing negatives ear/ego/ate. bin is e,r,p,m-free but introduces i not shown in positives.

Current round convergence: colleagues 1,10,11,12,13,14,2,3,4,5,6,7,8 all name A. leg as the canonical non-follower. The coda-set/2nd-position argument is used to show rat is actually the best follower, which is why C cannot be the "does not follow" answer; if rat is the inlier, picking C would leave four non-followers. The vowel rule gives a unique outlier: dam/rat/car/bin share a single non-E vowel, leg alone has E.

My current belief: the intended single-answer interpretation is the vowel/E rule, and leg is the textbook illustration of a break – CVC with forbidden vowel e in 2nd place and forbidden coda g in 3rd place, the same kind of violation as the negatives. I retain the caveat that under the strict 2nd∈{a,o,u}∧3rd∈{b,d,t,w} formalisation rat is the unique member of A–E and leg/dam/car/bin are all non-members, with leg remaining the canonical non-member to name. Unresolved tension between strict positional vs vowel-only formulation, but deliberation now strongly favours naming leg as the non-follower, and consensus in round 3 is A. leg.

